1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:02,139
Hey everyone, thanks for being here.

2
00:00:02,172 --> 00:00:02,958
I'm Arpita.

3
00:00:02,985 --> 00:00:04,357
I'm a staff AI engineer.

4
00:00:04,703 --> 00:00:08,736
today I want to talk about a shift
that I'm seeing across the industry

5
00:00:08,747 --> 00:00:10,811
in how teams handle AI compliance.

6
00:00:11,298 --> 00:00:14,790
this is my personal take on
where the industry is heading and

7
00:00:14,790 --> 00:00:16,518
not any one company's playbook.

8
00:00:16,528 --> 00:00:19,078
So everything I'm sharing
comes from public regulations

9
00:00:19,078 --> 00:00:20,479
and common industry practice.

10
00:00:20,899 --> 00:00:24,205
so yeah, let's begin with a
quick thought experiment, right?

11
00:00:24,524 --> 00:00:27,991
imagine an auditor emails you
tomorrow with three questions.

12
00:00:28,256 --> 00:00:31,820
What data trained the model that
you have in production right now?

13
00:00:32,073 --> 00:00:32,944
Who approved it?

14
00:00:32,944 --> 00:00:35,023
And how has it behaved since launch?

15
00:00:35,471 --> 00:00:37,126
How long would it take you to answer?

16
00:00:37,230 --> 00:00:39,823
For a lot of teams, it's
days, sometimes weeks.

17
00:00:40,160 --> 00:00:43,001
The gap is what we are
going to dig into today.

18
00:00:44,662 --> 00:00:49,735
So let me begin, let me make this
more concrete, right, with an example.

19
00:00:49,751 --> 00:00:52,365
So I'll use that example
throughout the talk.

20
00:00:52,738 --> 00:00:56,047
say your team runs a model that
helps screen job applications.

21
00:00:56,439 --> 00:00:59,115
Before launch, it went
through a proper review.

22
00:00:59,509 --> 00:01:03,536
Legal signed off, and bias tests
looked fine, and everything felt good.

23
00:01:03,894 --> 00:01:05,255
Then the model goes live.

24
00:01:05,557 --> 00:01:08,524
Every week, it retrains
on new applications.

25
00:01:08,534 --> 00:01:13,257
Someone on the other team, let's
say, changes how years of experience

26
00:01:13,260 --> 00:01:14,942
is calculated in the feature store.

27
00:01:15,327 --> 00:01:18,984
Now, nobody calls that as a release,
so nobody calls the review team.

28
00:01:19,386 --> 00:01:23,322
and six months later, the model in
production isn't the model that you

29
00:01:23,324 --> 00:01:25,654
reviewed or the, that was reviewed, right?

30
00:01:26,090 --> 00:01:27,682
It just has the same name.

31
00:01:27,988 --> 00:01:32,069
So that's the problem with treating
compliance as a one-time sign-off.

32
00:01:32,450 --> 00:01:37,639
The review was accurate when it happened,
but it stopped being true the week after.

33
00:01:37,922 --> 00:01:41,088
So the n-newer rules don't
ask, "Did you check once?"

34
00:01:41,114 --> 00:01:45,516
They ask more regularly as to, "Can
you show me that this is still okay?"

35
00:01:46,144 --> 00:01:49,919
so that brings me to,
so my core idea, right?

36
00:01:49,945 --> 00:01:51,529
The core idea here is simple.

37
00:01:52,110 --> 00:01:56,624
we stop treating compliance as a document
and start treating it like a code, right?

38
00:01:56,945 --> 00:01:59,215
it's not something that we
write once at the beginning.

39
00:01:59,216 --> 00:02:01,396
It's something that we refine over time.

40
00:02:01,916 --> 00:02:05,557
we already know how to keep software
from breaking as it changes, right?

41
00:02:05,584 --> 00:02:08,210
We write tests and run
them on every change.

42
00:02:08,730 --> 00:02:12,617
nobody runs unit tests once and before
launch and calls it done, right?

43
00:02:13,030 --> 00:02:14,506
it's the same thing with compliance.

44
00:02:14,524 --> 00:02:16,311
Compliance can work the same way.

45
00:02:16,735 --> 00:02:20,961
know what data you trained on becomes a
record the pipeline writes automatically.

46
00:02:21,388 --> 00:02:24,595
and check for bias becomes a
test with a pass mark, right?

47
00:02:24,924 --> 00:02:28,630
So you keep watching, it
basically becomes an alert.

48
00:02:28,940 --> 00:02:31,928
The policy is the rule, and the
pipeline produces the proof.

49
00:02:32,404 --> 00:02:35,590
s- so why does this matter now?

50
00:02:36,156 --> 00:02:37,523
There are three reasons.

51
00:02:37,895 --> 00:02:40,414
and I will keep the legal part short.

52
00:02:40,430 --> 00:02:45,873
So first, the EU AI Act shortens AI
systems by risk, and something like

53
00:02:45,873 --> 00:02:47,770
our hiring model counts as high risk.

54
00:02:48,124 --> 00:02:51,395
For those systems, the law asks
for things that sound a lot

55
00:02:51,422 --> 00:02:53,154
like an MLOps backlog, right?

56
00:02:53,515 --> 00:02:57,451
Manage risk, know your data, keep
technical documentation, log what the

57
00:02:57,451 --> 00:03:01,245
system does, keep a human involved,
and keep monitoring after launch.

58
00:03:01,621 --> 00:03:04,975
This year, the EU published the
deadlines for high-risk systems

59
00:03:05,043 --> 00:03:07,582
out to late 2027 and 2028.

60
00:03:08,041 --> 00:03:10,929
Some teams heard that as, "We have time."

61
00:03:11,304 --> 00:03:15,619
I would hear it as, "We have time to build
this properly instead of in a panic."

62
00:03:16,401 --> 00:03:19,714
Now second, in the US, right,
which is more closer to home,

63
00:03:19,721 --> 00:03:21,897
there is no single national AI law.

64
00:03:22,237 --> 00:03:26,006
Many companies use the NIST AI
Risk Management Framework as

65
00:03:26,009 --> 00:03:27,902
their guide, which is voluntary.

66
00:03:28,278 --> 00:03:31,437
States are writing their own rules,
and those rules keep changing.

67
00:03:31,796 --> 00:03:35,235
Colorado passed an AI law in twenty
twenty-four and replaced it this

68
00:03:35,236 --> 00:03:36,960
year before it ever took effect.

69
00:03:37,423 --> 00:03:40,051
When the rules keep moving,
you don't want to rebuild your

70
00:03:40,073 --> 00:03:41,947
evidence by hand every time.

71
00:03:42,608 --> 00:03:46,817
and third, privacy laws like GDPR,
which have been around for years, right?

72
00:03:46,834 --> 00:03:49,673
If someone asks you to delete
their data, you need to know

73
00:03:49,686 --> 00:03:51,113
which models learn from it.

74
00:03:51,576 --> 00:03:55,613
That's a data history question, and
most teams can't answer it quickly.

75
00:03:56,712 --> 00:04:00,405
So, so there are these
five pillars, right?

76
00:04:00,408 --> 00:04:02,199
Here's how I break the approach down.

77
00:04:02,697 --> 00:04:07,291
Read it from the bottom up because
each layer depends on the one below it.

78
00:04:07,711 --> 00:04:10,427
First, you know how risky a model is.

79
00:04:10,845 --> 00:04:13,258
Then you record where its data came from.

80
00:04:13,619 --> 00:04:15,622
Then you use the record to run checks.

81
00:04:15,689 --> 00:04:18,201
Then you keep watching
the model in production.

82
00:04:18,528 --> 00:04:21,926
And on top, documentation gets
built from everything underneath.

83
00:04:22,393 --> 00:04:24,637
None of this needs new infrastructure.

84
00:04:24,994 --> 00:04:28,752
You already have a model registry,
metadata, CI/CD, and monitoring.

85
00:04:28,760 --> 00:04:33,661
This is about connecting all
those dots together Now let's

86
00:04:33,663 --> 00:04:34,980
go to the first pillar, right?

87
00:04:34,980 --> 00:04:36,387
Which is risk classification.

88
00:04:36,396 --> 00:04:37,908
Let's dive deeper into each of these.

89
00:04:38,377 --> 00:04:42,089
So for layer one, we decide
how risky a model is in the

90
00:04:42,089 --> 00:04:43,744
moment it's registered, right?

91
00:04:44,255 --> 00:04:45,732
let's go back to our hiring model.

92
00:04:45,834 --> 00:04:48,824
When the team registers it, they
answer a few simple questions.

93
00:04:49,171 --> 00:04:50,357
What is it used for?

94
00:04:50,680 --> 00:04:52,674
Does it use personal or sensitive data?

95
00:04:52,707 --> 00:04:56,769
Does it make the final call on a person or
just suggest something or human reviews?

96
00:04:57,301 --> 00:04:59,961
From those answers, the
model gets a risk level.

97
00:05:00,474 --> 00:05:03,708
Hiring plus a decision about
a person means high risk.

98
00:05:04,374 --> 00:05:08,836
A model that recommends articles
is to pro-- to read probably

99
00:05:08,836 --> 00:05:10,345
isn't as high risk, right?

100
00:05:10,703 --> 00:05:11,706
So why start here?

101
00:05:12,252 --> 00:05:14,623
Because everything else
depends on this label.

102
00:05:14,911 --> 00:05:18,463
High risk models get more
checks, more documentation,

103
00:05:18,485 --> 00:05:19,997
and a required human sign-off.

104
00:05:20,486 --> 00:05:24,013
so the one lesson, one lesson that
you can take from here is keep

105
00:05:24,014 --> 00:05:28,950
this as clear rules with a person
performing or confirming the result.

106
00:05:29,287 --> 00:05:33,891
If a model gets labeled low risk by
mistake, every check after it quietly

107
00:05:33,891 --> 00:05:36,091
switches off and nobody notices.

108
00:05:37,040 --> 00:05:40,318
now to the second pillar,
which is lineage capture.

109
00:05:40,758 --> 00:05:45,215
layer two is lineage, which just means
a record of where everything came from.

110
00:05:45,690 --> 00:05:50,374
Every time a model trains, the pipeline
writes down which data it used and

111
00:05:50,374 --> 00:05:53,949
which version of it, which features
and how they were calculated, which

112
00:05:53,963 --> 00:05:56,010
code, and what the results were.

113
00:05:56,534 --> 00:05:59,642
The key is that this record is
written while training happens.

114
00:05:59,668 --> 00:06:03,389
It isn't pieced together later
from memory and old chat threads.

115
00:06:03,994 --> 00:06:08,101
We also attach privacy details to
the data, what people agreed to,

116
00:06:08,102 --> 00:06:09,937
and when the data has to be deleted.

117
00:06:10,339 --> 00:06:11,906
Here is the payoff of this.

118
00:06:12,246 --> 00:06:13,961
Remember the deletion request?

119
00:06:14,307 --> 00:06:17,780
With lineage, which models
use this person's data becomes

120
00:06:17,780 --> 00:06:19,115
a query that takes seconds.

121
00:06:19,152 --> 00:06:22,028
Without it, it's an
investigation that takes weeks.

122
00:06:22,573 --> 00:06:27,403
so one simple thing to, probably take
from here is make it as required, right?

123
00:06:27,406 --> 00:06:31,267
If a training run doesn't record
its lineage, the run kind of fails.

124
00:06:31,913 --> 00:06:34,647
Now the, to the third pillar,
which is policy gates.

125
00:06:35,291 --> 00:06:39,378
Layer three is where it starts to feel
like engineering, which is gates, right?

126
00:06:39,407 --> 00:06:43,978
A gate is an automatic check that can stop
the pipeline, and there are two of them.

127
00:06:44,425 --> 00:06:48,458
The first runs before training,
so, pre-training gate.

128
00:06:48,927 --> 00:06:51,366
Are we allowed to use the
data for this purpose?

129
00:06:51,675 --> 00:06:55,071
Did any unexpected personal
information sneak into the data set?

130
00:06:55,097 --> 00:06:57,393
Is any of it past its delete by date?

131
00:06:57,994 --> 00:06:59,843
things like that go in
the pre-training gate.

132
00:07:00,144 --> 00:07:03,886
The second one runs after
training and before deployment.

133
00:07:04,028 --> 00:07:07,532
Does the model meet the fairness
bar we set for this use case?

134
00:07:07,593 --> 00:07:09,182
Is the documentation complete?

135
00:07:09,232 --> 00:07:13,470
And for high-risk models like our
hiring one, has a person signed off

136
00:07:13,538 --> 00:07:15,990
with that, with that approval recorded?

137
00:07:16,511 --> 00:07:20,391
So if a gate fails, the deployment
stops the same way a failing

138
00:07:20,434 --> 00:07:21,844
test blocks a code merge.

139
00:07:22,317 --> 00:07:24,156
But here's something I
learned the hard way.

140
00:07:24,557 --> 00:07:27,945
A gate that just says policy
violation is kind of useless.

141
00:07:28,259 --> 00:07:31,403
If engineers don't know why it
failed or how to fix it, they

142
00:07:31,403 --> 00:07:32,831
will find a way around it.

143
00:07:33,290 --> 00:07:38,415
Every failure needs to say what
went wrong and what to do next That

144
00:07:38,417 --> 00:07:41,346
brings me to the fourth pillar,
which is monitoring signals.

145
00:07:41,735 --> 00:07:44,927
so layer four is watching
the model after it ships.

146
00:07:45,410 --> 00:07:47,295
There are three types of signals.

147
00:07:47,879 --> 00:07:49,925
the first is drift.

148
00:07:50,057 --> 00:07:53,945
If incoming data starts to look
different from what the model learned

149
00:07:53,965 --> 00:07:58,696
on, say, the job market shifts and
applicants look different, we raise

150
00:07:58,696 --> 00:08:00,507
an alert and save a dated record.

151
00:08:00,815 --> 00:08:03,687
That record is your proof that
you were paying attention.

152
00:08:04,062 --> 00:08:06,087
The second is fairness in production.

153
00:08:06,580 --> 00:08:11,393
that the tests before launch aren't enough
because real world results can change.

154
00:08:11,817 --> 00:08:15,800
One caution here is there are several
ways to measure fairness, and they

155
00:08:15,821 --> 00:08:17,519
often contradict with each other.

156
00:08:17,576 --> 00:08:20,836
Pick the one that fits your
use case and write down why.

157
00:08:21,307 --> 00:08:24,831
Measuring fairness also often
needs sensitive data like gender

158
00:08:24,834 --> 00:08:28,848
or age, so work on it with your
privacy team, not around them.

159
00:08:29,547 --> 00:08:31,410
The third is prediction logs.

160
00:08:31,676 --> 00:08:36,103
Save a sample of what the model decided
and which version made the decision.

161
00:08:36,468 --> 00:08:39,989
Just put a delete date on those
logs too, or your audit trail

162
00:08:40,016 --> 00:08:42,160
becomes its own privacy problem.

163
00:08:43,160 --> 00:08:46,853
And last and the most important
one, that's documentation.

164
00:08:46,861 --> 00:08:48,899
Layer five is the payoff, right?

165
00:08:49,221 --> 00:08:52,548
If the first four layers are doing
their job, you already have almost

166
00:08:52,574 --> 00:08:54,627
everything a compliance document needs.

167
00:08:55,100 --> 00:09:00,011
The risk level comes from registration,
the data source comes from lineage, the

168
00:09:00,011 --> 00:09:03,270
test results from the gates, and the
production behavior from monitoring.

169
00:09:03,611 --> 00:09:07,316
So instead of an engineer spending two
weeks writing a document that's out of

170
00:09:07,316 --> 00:09:11,893
date by the next retrain, the pipeline
fills in a template automatically.

171
00:09:11,922 --> 00:09:15,420
Every new model version gets
its own current document.

172
00:09:16,171 --> 00:09:19,186
And I want to be careful
here though, right?

173
00:09:19,213 --> 00:09:21,183
The pipeline fills in the facts.

174
00:09:21,507 --> 00:09:24,619
Some parts still need a person,
like explaining what the model is

175
00:09:24,629 --> 00:09:26,417
for and how people can step in.

176
00:09:26,979 --> 00:09:31,400
So automation handles the facts so
people can focus on the judgment calls

177
00:09:32,400 --> 00:09:37,937
No, for some, Now, everything so far
works for classic ML models, but a

178
00:09:37,937 --> 00:09:40,255
lot of you are shipping LLM features.

179
00:09:40,611 --> 00:09:41,657
So what changes?

180
00:09:42,000 --> 00:09:43,801
Let's go back to our hiring example.

181
00:09:43,820 --> 00:09:47,875
Say the team adds an LLM that summarizes
each resume for the recruiter.

182
00:09:48,351 --> 00:09:53,242
The same five layers still apply,
but what goes into each one changes.

183
00:09:53,570 --> 00:09:54,813
Lineage gets bigger.

184
00:09:54,831 --> 00:09:57,080
It's not just about training data anymore.

185
00:09:57,095 --> 00:10:01,959
You need to record which base model
you're using, which version, any data

186
00:10:01,966 --> 00:10:06,056
you fine-tuned it on, which version
of the prompt went out, and which

187
00:10:06,071 --> 00:10:08,471
documents it pulled in to answer.

188
00:10:08,940 --> 00:10:13,267
A small prompt edit can change the model's
behavior as much as a retrain, so treat

189
00:10:13,271 --> 00:10:15,111
prompts like code and version them.

190
00:10:15,649 --> 00:10:17,018
Gates, change too.

191
00:10:17,107 --> 00:10:19,022
One fairness number isn't enough.

192
00:10:19,046 --> 00:10:21,891
You run a test suite before every release.

193
00:10:21,951 --> 00:10:23,660
Does it produce harmful content?

194
00:10:23,660 --> 00:10:25,580
Does it leak personal information?

195
00:10:25,587 --> 00:10:29,449
Can someone trick it into
ignoring its instructions?

196
00:10:29,449 --> 00:10:30,537
Does it make things up?

197
00:10:30,630 --> 00:10:31,829
Same idea as before.

198
00:10:31,856 --> 00:10:33,522
If it fails, it doesn't ship.

199
00:10:34,099 --> 00:10:40,108
And monitoring means sampling real
conversations and outputs because LLMs can

200
00:10:40,143 --> 00:10:42,339
behave in ways your tests never imagined.

201
00:10:43,007 --> 00:10:44,955
And the rules have caught up here first.

202
00:10:44,969 --> 00:10:49,165
In Europe, obligations for companies
that build general purpose AI models have

203
00:10:49,165 --> 00:10:50,906
applied since August twenty twenty-five.

204
00:10:51,482 --> 00:10:54,761
Since this August, AI-generated
content generally has to

205
00:10:54,764 --> 00:10:56,556
be labeled as AI-generated.

206
00:10:57,003 --> 00:11:01,467
So for GenAI, a lot of this is already
in effect, not just planned for later

207
00:11:02,467 --> 00:11:05,569
A few lessons from building
systems like this at scale.

208
00:11:06,064 --> 00:11:08,130
First, start at registration.

209
00:11:08,270 --> 00:11:12,618
It's tempting to start with the
exciting part like a buyer's dashboard,

210
00:11:12,693 --> 00:11:16,868
but without a reliable risk label,
your gates don't know what to check.

211
00:11:17,287 --> 00:11:20,255
Second, record your data
history from day one.

212
00:11:20,344 --> 00:11:24,766
Adding it later to models that are
already running is slow and expensive.

213
00:11:25,214 --> 00:11:28,462
It's like trying to find the receipt
for something you bought a year ago.

214
00:11:29,171 --> 00:11:33,646
Third, and people underestimate
this one a lot, the thresholds

215
00:11:33,646 --> 00:11:34,952
are a negotiation, right?

216
00:11:35,210 --> 00:11:38,085
So when compliance sets them
alone, they tend to be too strict,

217
00:11:38,136 --> 00:11:41,257
and engineers quietly build side
pipelines to get around them.

218
00:11:41,613 --> 00:11:44,533
When engineering sets them alone,
they tend to be too loose, and

219
00:11:44,533 --> 00:11:46,018
you are exposed in an audit.

220
00:11:46,486 --> 00:11:51,416
The thresholds that last are the
ones that both teams agreed on Now,

221
00:11:51,438 --> 00:11:53,150
some practical trade-offs, right?

222
00:11:53,212 --> 00:11:56,794
This-- obviously, all of this
that we discussed isn't free.

223
00:11:56,905 --> 00:12:01,128
Gates will slow teams down sometimes,
and a strict consent check can

224
00:12:01,130 --> 00:12:04,815
block legitimate research, and
one fairness bar for every model

225
00:12:04,845 --> 00:12:06,163
will break for some of them.

226
00:12:06,644 --> 00:12:08,041
So a few things help here.

227
00:12:08,529 --> 00:12:12,035
give compliance the same dashboard
that engineers see so nobody's

228
00:12:12,049 --> 00:12:13,278
working from screenshots.

229
00:12:13,288 --> 00:12:14,888
Have an exception path, right?

230
00:12:15,311 --> 00:12:16,734
and this is the most important one.

231
00:12:17,070 --> 00:12:20,303
exception path is basically a
temporary override with an expiry

232
00:12:20,306 --> 00:12:24,157
date that gets logged, so the
exception itself becomes evidence.

233
00:12:24,584 --> 00:12:28,017
Review the thresholds regularly
because the rules keep changing.

234
00:12:28,067 --> 00:12:32,357
And if you can, put someone who
understands compliance inside the platform

235
00:12:32,359 --> 00:12:34,662
team, not just at the end as a reviewer.

236
00:12:35,662 --> 00:12:37,121
now for some key takeaways.

237
